{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And after you</b> — 'And after you shall arise another kingdom inferior to yours,' as the Targum [renders] 'lower.' And because of its lowness the earth is called ארעא, for it is below everything. The Gaon [Saadia] said, and upon him all the commentators relied: the gold is the kingdom of Babylon — Nebuchadnezzar and his son Evil-merodach and Belshazzar his grandson, and with him the kingdom of the Chaldeans ceased, as it is written: 'בהליליא קטיל בלשצר מלכא' (in that night Belshazzar the king was killed), and 'Darius the Mede received the kingdom.' And the matter is correct, for Scripture says: 'and the nations shall serve him and his son and his son's son.' And it is further written: 'and they shall serve him and his sons,' who are Evil-merodach and Belshazzar his son, for the son's son is called 'son,' as in 'El Reuel their father' — for he is Hobab, father-in-law of Moses. And it is written: 'And it came to pass in the thirty-seventh year of the exile of Jehoiachin king of Judah, Evil-merodach king of Babylon lifted up [the head of Jehoiachin],' and he is between the kingdom of Nebuchadnezzar and the kingdom of Belshazzar." "<b>And the silver</b> — the kingdom of Media and Persia. And I shall yet explain it, for there is a great error in their numbering." "<b>And the bronze</b> — it is the kingdom of Greece, and their beginning is Alexander of Macedon, as Daniel shall explain in his words of prophecy. And the Gaon said: the fourth is the kingdom of Aram. And 'behold, it shall be strong' is the kingdom of Ishmael, and he found no mention of it in the book of Daniel, though it is greater in length and breadth than all the kingdoms mentioned. And he said: the kingdom of Aram is like iron, and the potter's clay is the kingdom of Ishmael. And he further said in his book: do not wonder that he compared the kingdom of Ishmael to clay, for it is not inferior to the kingdoms mentioned — only thus is the manner of arranging his vessels: first the gold is shown, then the silver, then the bronze, then the iron, and last a vessel of clay. And these are words of tohu and bohu, for there is no proper order to his arrangement. The proof: Daniel said of the kingdom of Persia 'inferior to yours' (ארע מנך), just as silver is inferior to gold; and he said of the clay 'and it shall be broken' (ומנה תהוי תבירה). And the reliable proof is that the clay is of the iron kingdom, for he explicitly said of 'the iron toes and the clay toes': 'part of the kingdom shall be strong (תקיפא) and part broken (תבירא).' And behold, it is one kingdom — so how can the kingdom of Ishmael be joined with the kingdom of Aram, when Ishmael never ruled over it, and their Torah is not one?! And since the matter is thus, one must wonder how Daniel did not mention the kingdom of Ishmael, and why there were not five kingdoms. And now I shall explain: Know that Alexander, who was king of Greece, is the one who killed Darius the Persian, as Daniel shall explain. He was also king of Aram, and Aram itself is Kittim, as Joseph ben Gurion showed in his book, and in Daniel: 'and he shall come against the ships of Kittim' (ובא בו ציים כתים). And it appears as possible, and the Targum of 'and Kittim' is 'from the direction of Italy shall they be sent forth' (מן רומאי אשתלחן). And behold, Kittim is the son of Greece: Elishah and Tarshish, Kittim and Dodanim. If so, the kingdom of bronze is the kingdom of Greece, and the kingdom of Aram is the kingdom of Greece. If so, the iron is the kingdom of Ishmael. And because the kingdom of Persia destroyed the entire kingdom of the Chaldeans, and the kingdom of Greece also destroyed the entire kingdom of Persia — but the kingdom of Ishmael, which is the iron, could not destroy the kingdom of Greece except for part of the kingdom. And behold, to this day there are places where Ishmael prevails over the kingdom of Aram, and places where Ishmael is defeated. Therefore he said: 'part of the kingdom shall be broken and part shall be strong' (מקצת מלכותא תהוי תבירה ומקצת תהוי תקיפה). And he said afterward: 'and in the days of those kings' (וביומיהון די מלכיא אינון) — the meaning is two kingdoms ruled Aram, which are the kingdom of Greece and the kingdom of Ishmael, and these two kingdoms exist to this day. And 'it shall rule as the fourth' (ומלכת רביעאה) — this is the kingdom of Ishmael that spread in the world, for they returned to their Torah: all of Persia and Media and Sheba and Saba and Egypt and Africa and many of India and most of the kingdom of Cush."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes the Gaon throughout; resolved as Saadia per standard epithet. The Aramaic citations from Daniel are left in Hebrew script per voice rules.", 1 "'Their numbering' likely refers to Ibn Ezra's dispute with other commentators over the enumeration of kingdoms; rendered literally without expansion.", 2 "The phrase תהו ובהו is rendered literally as 'tohu and bohu' per voice rules for cryptic/literal preservation. Joseph ben Gurion refers to the ancient historian Yosippon; left as 'Joseph ben Gurion' without resolution."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:19:41.085709089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}